class:: TuningFrequency
summary:: Circular estimate of tuning relative to A440
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Tonal
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/HPCP, Classes/HarmonicPeaks

description::
Computes the circular mean of spectral-peak semitone residuals, weighted by squared peak magnitude. This follows the tuning-aware tonal analysis principle of Gomez and Bonada (ICMC 2005); the circular estimator is an explicit MIR2 convention. If r = 12 * log2(f/440), the angle is 2*pi*(r-round(r)). The angle of the weighted resultant gives detuning; its length divided by total weight gives concentration. Detuning is only identifiable modulo a semitone and is reported within approximately -50 through +50 cents. This measures agreement with an equal-tempered grid, not the correctness of a performance. Vibrato, inharmonic partials and non-equal-tempered music reduce its reliability.

Vanilla link::Classes/Pitch:: follows a monophonic frequency. This class estimates the common fractional-semitone offset of several spectral peaks.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: chain
FFT chain. This analyzer updates when a new frame arrives and holds its output between frames.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included spectral frequency in Hz.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included spectral frequency in Hz; zero means Nyquist.

argument:: peakThreshold
Minimum local-peak magnitude relative to the largest included spectral peak, from 0 through 1. Up to 128 strongest peaks are retained.

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192, matching the FFT buffer. Frequency resolution and first-result latency depend on this size.

returns::
An Array: estimated A reference in Hz, cents relative to A440, and circular concentration from 0 through 1. Silence returns zeros.

examples::
TuningFrequency calculates a circular weighted estimate of concert pitch relative to A440, returning estimated reference A (Hz), signed cents detuning, and circular concentration. In this example, it monitors acoustic piano tuning and tunes a reference test sine oscillator to demonstrate automatic calibration to non-standard concert pitch. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time estimated concert A in Hz.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("piano-chords.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic chords fallback
            Mix.ar(SinOsc.ar([261.63, 329.63, 392.00, 523.25] * LFNoise0.kr(0.5).range(1, 1.25))) * 0.15
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(2048), source, 0.25);
        var tuning = TuningFrequency.kr(chain, minFreq: 100, maxFreq: 6000, fftSize: 2048);
        var estA = tuning[0].lag(0.08);
        var refTone = SinOsc.ar(estA.clip(420, 460), 0, tuning[2].lag(0.08) * 0.05);
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + (refTone ! 2);

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, estA.clip(420, 460)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Concert A Tuning", ~mir2Bus, 430.0, 450.0, "Hz");
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

